<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1898)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Paris of December 1898 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, which came under American military government, and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the last for 20 million dollars. It ended Spain's empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and made the United States a colonial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898.jpg" alt="Treaty of Paris (1898)" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frances Benjamin Johnston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jules_Cambon_signs_Treaty_of_Paris,_1899.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Spanish–American War, Spain ceded Cuba, Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines to the United States by the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1898 for US$20 million, and Cuba, which had declared independence from the Spanish Empire that year, passed under an American military government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain ceded the islands to the United States in the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Paris</b> ceded the island to the United States, and military rule gave way to civil government under the Foraker Act of 1900, with an appointed governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States refused to recognise the government, and the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of December 1898 transferred the islands from Spain to America.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Grito de Lares revolt of 1868, Spain granted autonomy in 1897, but the United States invaded in 1898 during the Spanish-American War, and the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> transferred the island to Puerto Rico (United States).</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-puerto-rico">Spanish Puerto Rico</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States entered the Cuban War of Independence in April 1898, destroyed the Spanish fleet off Santiago de Cuba in July and forced Spain to give up Cuba in the <b>Treaty of Paris</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-military-government-in-cuba">United States Military Government in Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
